(
  gen_random_uuid(),
  'Guide to ISRC and UPC codes for independent musicians',
  'guide-to-isrc-and-upc-codes',
  'Learn what ISRC and UPC codes are, why they are essential for tracking your royalties across Spotify, Apple Music and YouTube, and how Musicdibs manages this metadata for you during distribution.',
  $HTML$<p>If you are preparing to release music as an independent artist, you have probably come across two mysterious acronyms: <strong>ISRC</strong> and <strong>UPC</strong>. Both are industry-standard identifiers, and both are essential to make sure every stream, download and sync placement is correctly attributed to you. This guide explains what they are, how they work, and how <strong>Musicdibs</strong> handles them automatically so you can focus on the music.</p><h2>What is an ISRC code?</h2><p>The <strong>International Standard Recording Code (ISRC)</strong> is a 12-character identifier assigned to a specific sound recording or music video. It is standardized by ISO (ISO 3901) and administered globally by the IFPI. Each ISRC is unique to one recording — if you release a remix, a live version or a re-master, each one gets its own ISRC.</p><p>An ISRC looks like this: <code>ES-A2B-25-00001</code>, where:</p><ul><li><strong>ES</strong> — country code (Spain in this example).</li><li><strong>A2B</strong> — registrant code assigned to the label or distributor.</li><li><strong>25</strong> — year of registration (2025).</li><li><strong>00001</strong> — designation code, unique per recording.</li></ul><h2>What is a UPC code?</h2><p>The <strong>Universal Product Code (UPC)</strong>, sometimes referred to as EAN in Europe, is a 12- or 13-digit barcode that identifies a <strong>release as a product</strong>: a single, EP or album. While an ISRC identifies one recording, a UPC identifies the entire package that contains one or more recordings.</p><p>Every commercial release needs its own UPC so that streaming platforms, retailers and neighbouring-rights societies can distinguish it from any other product on the market.</p><h2>ISRC vs UPC: what is the difference?</h2><ul><li><strong>ISRC</strong> = one specific recording (a single track).</li><li><strong>UPC</strong> = one commercial release (single, EP or album containing tracks).</li><li>An album with 10 tracks has <strong>1 UPC + 10 ISRCs</strong>.</li><li>If you re-release the same album in a deluxe edition, you need a <strong>new UPC</strong> and, for any new or re-recorded tracks, <strong>new ISRCs</strong>.</li></ul><h2>Why ISRC and UPC codes matter for your royalties</h2><p>These identifiers are the backbone of the modern royalty pipeline. Without them:</p><ul><li>Streaming platforms cannot report your plays to distributors and rights societies.</li><li>Neighbouring-rights collection agencies (AIE, PPL, SoundExchange, etc.) cannot pay you for radio and public performance.</li><li>Sync placements in films, series or ads cannot be tracked and monetized.</li><li>Your release cannot appear in Spotify for Artists or Apple Music for Artists analytics.</li></ul><p>In short: <strong>no ISRC and UPC = no royalties</strong>. Every professional release must have them.</p><h2>How to get ISRC codes for your music</h2><ol><li><strong>Through your distributor.</strong> The easiest and most common option. When you upload a release, the distributor assigns valid ISRCs and a UPC automatically at no extra cost.</li><li><strong>Direct registration.</strong> You can apply for a registrant code with your national IFPI office and manage your own ISRCs. This is only worth it if you release dozens of recordings per year.</li><li><strong>Legacy imports.</strong> If your recordings already have ISRCs from a previous release, you can (and should) reuse them so historical royalties are not lost.</li></ol><h2>How Musicdibs manages ISRC and UPC for you</h2><p>When you distribute a release through <strong>Musicdibs</strong>, the platform handles the entire metadata layer for you:</p><ul><li><strong>Automatic ISRC assignment</strong> for every new recording, using standardized codes accepted by all major DSPs.</li><li><strong>Automatic UPC assignment</strong> for each release, so your single, EP or album is uniquely identifiable across stores.</li><li><strong>Preservation of existing codes</strong>: if your recording already has an ISRC, you can enter it and Musicdibs will keep it, ensuring continuity of royalty tracking.</li><li><strong>Blockchain-certified ownership</strong>: on top of the metadata, Musicdibs registers your work on blockchain to prove authorship and date of creation.</li><li><strong>Zero commission on streaming royalties</strong>: Musicdibs does not charge any commission on your streaming royalties. Every euro collected by DSPs against your ISRCs comes back to you.</li></ul><h2>Best practices for metadata as an independent artist</h2><ul><li>Use one ISRC per recording, and never reuse it for a different version.</li><li>Store your ISRCs and UPCs in a safe place — a spreadsheet or a dedicated section in your artist dashboard.</li><li>Register your works before uploading them to any distributor to have a timestamped proof of authorship.</li><li>Update contributors, songwriters and splits before release: fixing metadata later is painful and often blocks royalty payments.</li></ul><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Do I have to pay for an ISRC?</h3><p>No. When you distribute through Musicdibs, ISRCs and UPCs are included with no extra fee.</p><h3>Can I change an ISRC once assigned?</h3><p>No. An ISRC is permanent for a given recording. If you significantly re-record or remix a song, that new version gets its own ISRC.</p><h3>Do live versions and remixes need their own ISRC?</h3><p>Yes. Any distinct recording — live, acoustic, remix, radio edit — must have a unique ISRC.</p><h3>Is a UPC the same as a barcode?</h3><p>Functionally yes: a UPC is a barcode format used to identify a commercial release. For digital releases you rarely see the barcode itself, but the number is what DSPs and retailers use internally.</p><h3>What happens if I change distributor?</h3><p>Your ISRCs travel with your recordings. Your new distributor should preserve them so streaming history and royalty pipelines remain intact.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>ISRC and UPC codes are invisible to fans but critical for artists: they are the fingerprint that connects every stream, download and sync to your bank account. With <strong>Musicdibs</strong>, metadata management is fully automated — you get proper identifiers, blockchain-certified ownership and 0% commission on your streaming royalties, all from a single dashboard.</p>$HTML$,
